// HARD mode · Biblical teaching — English version.
// Same order and same answer order as data/dificil.js (correct index is taken from the Spanish file; the game shuffles the options).
// Each entry: [section, question, [answers], explanation, [paraphrased text of each reference]]
window.QUESTIONS_HARD_EN = [
["Gospels · Jesus","What does it mean that Jesus is “the way, the truth and the life”?",[
 "Jesus came to show us the way to God; if we follow his teachings well enough, our obedience makes us worthy of reaching the Father.",
 "That Jesus is the one who reveals the Father to us, gives us life, and through him we can come to God.",
 "Jesus is one of the means God uses to bring us to himself; those who sincerely follow another path can also reach the Father without Christ."],
 "Jesus did not only say that he knew the way. He himself is the way. In him we truly know the Father and, through his work, we have access to God and receive life.",
 ["Jesus said that he is the way, the truth and the life, and that no one comes to the Father except through him. He also explained that knowing him is knowing the Father, because his words and works show the Father.","Because of what Jesus did, we can draw near to God with confidence, with a sincere heart full of faith."]],
["Gospels · New life","What does it mean to be born again?",[
 "Being born again happens when we decide to change our lives; our decision and effort produce that new spiritual life in us.",
 "Being born again means that God forgives the past and then we build, through obedience, the new life we need.",
 "To receive a new life from God through the work of his Spirit, by means of his Word."],
 "Being born again is God's work. It is not simply reforming our behavior or adopting religious habits. God gives new life, and that life begins to produce real transformation.",
 ["Jesus taught that no one can see the kingdom of God unless they are born again. This birth comes from the Spirit.","You have been born again, not of seed that perishes, but of seed that endures: the living word of God."]],
["Gospels · Salvation","Why did Jesus come into the world?",[
 "To seek and save the lost and give us life through him.",
 "Jesus came mainly to show us how to live; those who imitate his example well enough can be reconciled to God through that obedience.",
 "Jesus opened a new opportunity for salvation, but each person must complete their own rescue through a good enough life."],
 "Jesus came as Savior. His teaching matters, but his mission is not reduced to teaching us how to behave: he came to rescue the lost and reconcile us to God.",
 ["The Son of Man came to seek and to save the lost.","God loved the world and gave his Son so that whoever believes in him has eternal life. God did not send his Son to condemn the world, but so that the world might be saved through him."]],
["Gospels · Faith","According to Jesus, what does it mean to believe in him?",[
 "Believing in Jesus consists in accepting his teachings as true; that intellectual agreement is enough even if we never personally depend on him.",
 "Believing means committing to obey Jesus until we prove through our works that we deserve to receive eternal life.",
 "To trust in Jesus and come to him to receive the life that God offers."],
 "Biblical faith is more than acknowledging a fact about Jesus. It is trusting him, coming to him, and resting in the salvation God offers through his Son.",
 ["Jesus presented himself as the bread of life. Whoever comes to him will never go hungry, and whoever believes in him will never be thirsty. He will never drive away anyone who comes to him.","God so loved the world that he gave his one and only Son, that whoever believes in him shall not perish but have eternal life."]],
["Gospels · Repentance","What does it mean to repent?",[
 "Repenting means feeling enough sorrow for sin; that sorrow is what gets God to forgive us.",
 "To recognize our sin, turn to God, and respond in faith to his call.",
 "Repenting means correcting our behavior until we make up before God for the wrong we did before."],
 "Repentance includes recognizing that we have been going the wrong way and turning to God. It is not paying our debt with good works, but responding to God with faith and a real change of direction.",
 ["Jesus announced that the kingdom of God had come near and called people to repent and believe the good news.","The son recognized his condition, got up and went back to his father."]],
["Gospels · Discipleship","What does it mean to follow Jesus?",[
 "To recognize Jesus as Lord and learn to live following him, even when following him has a cost.",
 "Following Jesus means imitating his conduct well enough to become worthy of belonging to him.",
 "Following Jesus consists in keeping his teachings; as long as our obedience is sufficient, we do not need to depend on his grace."],
 "Following Jesus is not adding a religious activity to our schedule. It is recognizing his authority, learning from him, and orienting our life toward him.",
 ["Jesus said that whoever wants to follow him must deny themselves, take up their cross daily and follow him.","Jesus said that whoever serves him must follow him, and where he is, his servant will be too."]],
["Gospels · Kingdom","What is the kingdom of God in Jesus' teaching?",[
 "The kingdom of God is a society that human beings establish when they manage to apply God's laws correctly.",
 "God's rule and authority becoming present and calling people to respond to him.",
 "The kingdom of God comes when a community reaches enough obedience to establish God's rule on earth by itself."],
 "When Jesus announces the kingdom, he announces God's rule drawing near to people. The King is acting, saving, and calling us to live under his authority.",
 ["Jesus announced that the time had come and that the kingdom of God had come near.","Jesus explained that the kingdom of God does not come as a spectacle that can simply be pointed at; the kingdom was in their midst."]],
["Gospels · Grace","What does the parable of the Pharisee and the tax collector teach about approaching God?",[
 "God justifies the sinner when his repentance produces enough good works to prove that he deserves mercy.",
 "God's mercy completes the missing part after our good works supply most of the righteousness required.",
 "That God receives the one who recognizes his need and seeks his mercy, not the one who trusts in his own righteousness."],
 "Jesus confronts confidence in one's own righteousness. We do not come before God boasting of our merits, but recognizing our need for mercy.",
 ["A Pharisee presented his merits before God. The tax collector asked for mercy, acknowledging his sin. Jesus said that the latter went home justified."]],
["Gospels · Love","What did Jesus mean when he said we should love our enemies?",[
 "That we should seek the good even of those who mistreat us, without making hatred our response.",
 "Loving your enemy means not calling what they do sin, because pointing out their wrong would mean no longer loving them.",
 "Loving your enemy requires staying close to them and trusting them even if they keep harming us; setting boundaries contradicts Jesus' command."],
 "Loving our enemy does not mean approving evil or remaining defenseless against abuse. It means the evil we receive does not rule our response: we can seek justice without feeding hatred or revenge.",
 ["Jesus taught us to love our enemies and pray for those who persecute us, remembering that the Father shows kindness even to those who do not honor him."]],
["Gospels · Prayer","What does Jesus teach us about prayer?",[
 "God grants what we ask when we reach enough faith; an unanswered request usually shows that our faith was insufficient.",
 "Persistent prayer can force God to grant something he did not want to give if we show enough insistence and faith.",
 "That prayer is a sincere relationship with the Father, not a performance to impress others."],
 "Jesus presents prayer as trust in and dependence on the Father. We do not need to perform before God or manipulate him with words.",
 ["Jesus taught not to pray to impress others or to repeat words thinking we will be heard because of them. He taught us to address the Father and seek his name, his kingdom, his will, and our daily dependence on him."]],
["Gospels · Forgiveness","Why does forgiveness hold such an important place in the Christian life?",[
 "God forgives us because we first forgive others; our forgiveness of them is the cause of his forgiving us.",
 "Because whoever has received mercy from God is called to live showing mercy to others.",
 "Forgiving means that the offense no longer has consequences and that there can no longer be correction, restitution or boundaries."],
 "Forgiving does not turn evil into good or automatically remove its consequences. But whoever understands the mercy they have received from God cannot treat revenge as their normal way of life.",
 ["Jesus told about a servant who was forgiven an enormous debt but then refused to show mercy to another. The parable shows how serious it is to receive mercy and refuse to extend it."]],
["Gospels · Cross","Why is Jesus' death central to the Gospel?",[
 "Because by giving himself, Jesus did what was necessary for our rescue and forgiveness.",
 "The cross was mainly an example of obedience for us to imitate; by following that example well enough we can be saved.",
 "Jesus' death provides part of the forgiveness, and our good works must supply the rest for God to accept us."],
 "The cross was not simply a tragedy nor only an example of sacrifice. Jesus willingly gave his life to rescue and to forgive.",
 ["Jesus said that the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve and to give his life as a ransom for many.","Jesus spoke of his blood poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins."]],
["Gospels · Resurrection","Why does it matter that Jesus rose from the dead?",[
 "The resurrection teaches that God will give eternal life to those who have built up enough obedience, just as he rewarded Jesus' faithfulness.",
 "The main importance of the resurrection is to confirm that Jesus' moral teaching was right; our salvation does not depend on his actually rising.",
 "Because it confirms Christ's victory over death and is essential to our hope."],
 "The resurrection belongs to the heart of the Gospel. Christ defeated death, and our future hope is bound up with his victory.",
 ["The messengers announced that Jesus was not among the dead, but had risen, just as he had said.","Paul explains that if Christ has not been raised, faith would be futile; but Christ has truly been raised from the dead."]],
["Gospels · Who Jesus is","What does it mean to call Jesus “Lord”?",[
 "Recognizing Jesus as Lord means obeying his commands well enough to earn the right to belong to his kingdom.",
 "To recognize Jesus' authority and respond to him with faith and obedience.",
 "Jesus becomes our Lord only after our conduct proves that we are worthy of submitting to him."],
 "Calling Jesus Lord is not just saying a title. It is recognizing who he is and that his authority reaches our life.",
 ["Jesus asked why some called him Lord but did not do what he said.","God exalted Jesus, and the day will come when every knee will bow and every tongue will acknowledge that Jesus Christ is Lord."]],
["Gospels · Greatness","According to Jesus, what does it mean to be great?",[
 "Learning to serve, following Jesus' example.",
 "Greatness before God is earned by piling up acts of service; whoever serves more deserves a higher position in his kingdom.",
 "Jesus taught that all exercise of authority is contrary to service, so a true disciple must never lead or take authority over others."],
 "Jesus redefines greatness. In his kingdom, greatness does not consist in dominating others, but in using our life to serve.",
 ["Jesus contrasted the domination of rulers with his disciples: among them, whoever wants to be great must serve. He himself came to serve and to give his life."]],
["Acts · Holy Spirit","Why did God give the Holy Spirit to his disciples?",[
 "The Holy Spirit was given mainly as an extraordinary sign to identify who is spiritually superior within the church.",
 "The Spirit gives us power, but keeping our salvation depends on using that power correctly and maintaining enough obedience.",
 "To empower them to be witnesses of Christ and live as his people."],
 "The Spirit is not given as a badge of spiritual superiority. God empowers his people to bear witness to Christ and to live under his guidance.",
 ["Jesus promised that his disciples would receive power when the Holy Spirit came on them, and they would be his witnesses to the ends of the earth.","Peter announced that the risen and exalted Jesus received from the Father the promised Holy Spirit and poured him out on his disciples."]],
["Acts · Gospel","What was at the center of the first disciples' preaching?",[
 "Jesus, his death, his resurrection, and the salvation God offers through him.",
 "The apostles mainly preached Jesus' example: whoever manages to live by it can be reconciled to God.",
 "The apostles taught that Jesus' death began salvation, but our works must complete what is missing."],
 "The center of the apostolic message was Christ. The apostles proclaimed who Jesus is, what God did through his death and resurrection, and called people to respond to him.",
 ["Peter proclaimed Jesus: handed over to death, crucified, and raised by God.","Peter declared that salvation is found in Jesus and that no other name has been given by which we must be saved."]],
["Acts · Church","What do we see in the first Christian community about the life of the church?",[
 "Constantly taking part in the church's teaching, prayer and fellowship is what keeps the believer saved; stopping attendance makes them lose that salvation.",
 "The believers learned together, prayed, shared meals and cared for one another.",
 "The church owns and administers salvation, so a person can remain accepted by God only while formally remaining within it."],
 "In Acts, church means a community gathered around Christ. They learn the Word, pray, share, and care for one another.",
 ["The believers devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching, to fellowship, to the breaking of bread and to prayer. They shared and met one another's needs."]],
["Acts · Conversion","When people heard the Gospel, what response were they called to?",[
 "Repentance, faith and baptism each contribute a part of salvation; God cannot forgive until we complete all three requirements.",
 "Believing begins salvation, but baptism and an obedient life complete the righteousness God requires in order to accept us.",
 "To repent, believe in the Lord Jesus, and publicly express that faith."],
 "The Gospel calls for a response. The apostles called people to repent and believe in Christ. Baptism publicly accompanied that new identification with Jesus.",
 ["When they heard the message, they asked what they should do. Peter called them to repent and be baptized in the name of Jesus Christ.","When the jailer asked what he must do to be saved, Paul and Silas answered: believe in the Lord Jesus."]],
["Acts · Mission","Who is the good news of Jesus for?",[
 "For all nations; God calls people from every people to Christ.",
 "God offers different paths of salvation according to each culture; Jesus is only the path for those who know Christianity.",
 "The Gospel is for all nations, but only those who show enough obedience come to deserve the salvation it offers."],
 "Acts shows how the Gospel breaks down borders. Salvation in Christ does not belong to one ethnicity, nation or social class.",
 ["Jesus sent his witnesses from Jerusalem to the ends of the earth.","Peter understood that God does not show favoritism to one nation, but accepts people from every nation who seek him."]],
["Romans · Sin","What is the fundamental problem of human beings according to Romans?",[
 "Human beings are born morally neutral; the problem appears only when they pile up enough bad deeds to be separated from God.",
 "That all have sinned and need God's grace.",
 "Our main problem is ignorance: if we learn God's will correctly we can restore our relationship with him by ourselves."],
 "Paul puts everyone on the same ground: we all need grace. This removes both the despair of those who think they cannot be forgiven and the pride of those who believe they need no forgiveness.",
 ["There is no difference: all have sinned and fall short of the glory of God, and are justified freely by his grace through the redemption that came by Christ Jesus."]],
["Romans · Justification","What does it mean to be justified by faith?",[
 "God declares us righteous at first by faith, but afterward keeps that declaration only as long as our obedience reaches a sufficient level.",
 "God adds up our faith and our works; when together they reach the necessary righteousness, then he justifies us.",
 "That God declares us to be in a right relationship with him through faith in Christ, not because we have earned that place."],
 "Justification does not mean we will never go wrong again. It means our acceptance before God rests on Christ and is received by faith, not as wages for our merits.",
 ["Since we have been justified through faith, we have peace with God through our Lord Jesus Christ and access into this grace.","Paul concludes that a person is justified by faith apart from the works of the law."]],
["Romans · Grace","If we are saved by grace, does that mean we can deliberately live in sin and it doesn't matter?",[
 "No. The same grace that saves us unites us to Christ and calls us to a new life.",
 "Grace forgives our past sins, but from that moment on we must maintain our salvation through sufficient obedience.",
 "God supplies the grace that begins salvation and we supply the works that complete and secure it."],
 "Paul rejects two errors: thinking we can save ourselves by our works, and thinking grace makes the way we live irrelevant. Grace saves us and opens a new life in Christ.",
 ["Paul asks whether we should go on sinning so that grace may increase, and answers with a firm no. Those who were united with Christ were called to walk in a new life."]],
["Romans · Spirit","What role does the Holy Spirit have in the believer's life?",[
 "The Spirit can advise and strengthen us, but the transformation of the heart ultimately depends on our willpower.",
 "He dwells and works in the believer, guiding them toward a life that pleases God and helping them in their weakness.",
 "When the Spirit dwells in a person he removes the possibility of sinning again; if they sin again it shows the Spirit has left them."],
 "The Spirit works in the believer's everyday life. He guides us, helps us in our weakness, and produces a new orientation toward God.",
 ["Paul contrasts living ruled by the flesh with living according to the Spirit. Those who are led by the Spirit of God are children of God.","The Spirit also helps us in our weakness and intercedes when we do not know how to pray."]],
["Romans · Assurance","What does it mean that nothing can separate us from the love of God in Christ?",[
 "Nothing external can separate us from God's love, but every sin makes God stop loving us until we obey again.",
 "The promise means God will keep his children from suffering persecution, loss or truly painful circumstances.",
 "That even in real suffering, no created power can tear the believer away from God's love in Christ."],
 "The promise is not the absence of suffering. Paul names painful circumstances precisely and states that none of them can separate God's people from his love in Christ.",
 ["Paul lists trouble, persecution, danger, death and many other realities, and declares that nothing in all creation can separate us from the love of God that is in Christ Jesus."]],
["1 Corinthians · Cross","Why does Paul place the cross of Christ above human wisdom?",[
 "Because God's salvation does not depend on human intellectual pride, but on what God did in Christ.",
 "The cross shows that using reason is contrary to faith; the less we examine a religious teaching, the more we trust God.",
 "God chose the cross because studying the things of God deeply usually leads away from faith and should be avoided."],
 "Paul does not attack thinking. He attacks the claim to place human wisdom above God. Salvation is not our intellectual achievement, but God's work in Christ.",
 ["The message of the cross seems foolish to some, but to those who are being saved it is the power of God. God's wisdom surpasses human wisdom."]],
["1 Corinthians · Love","How does Paul describe Christian love?",[
 "Loving means approving the other person's choices even if they are sinful; calling something they do sin is incompatible with love.",
 "Love requires always placing another person's wishes above goodness and truth, even if those wishes end up harming them.",
 "As a way of living that seeks the good, rejoices with the truth, and does not revolve around oneself."],
 "Biblical love involves feelings, but it is not reduced to them. It is expressed in patience, kindness, truth, and a readiness to seek the other's good.",
 ["Love is patient and kind; it does not envy, it is not proud, it does not seek only its own, it does not delight in evil but rejoices with the truth."]],
["1 Corinthians · Gifts","Why does God give gifts to believers?",[
 "Spiritual gifts indicate each believer's rank; whoever receives a more visible gift is more important before God.",
 "To serve the common good and build up the body of Christ.",
 "Gifts are rewards for obedience: the more faithful a person is, the more important the gifts God is obliged to give them."],
 "Gifts are not spiritual trophies. God gives them for service. The variety of abilities should produce mutual care, not competition.",
 ["There are different gifts, services and kinds of working, but the same God works in all of them. To each one the manifestation of the Spirit is given for the common good.","God wants the members to care for one another; together they form the body of Christ."]],
["1 Corinthians · Resurrection","What hope does the believer have in the face of death?",[
 "That God will raise those who belong to Christ, and death will not have the last word.",
 "Christian hope is leaving the body for good to live forever as spirits; God has no future purpose for our bodies.",
 "Only believers who reach enough holiness will be raised; the rest of the saved will remain bodiless forever."],
 "Christian hope is not simply escaping the body. Jesus' resurrection points to our own resurrection: God will finally defeat death.",
 ["Christ has been raised from the dead as the firstfruits; those who belong to Christ will also be raised.","The dead will be raised imperishable, and death will finally be defeated."]],
["2 Corinthians · New creation","What does it mean to be a new creation in Christ?",[
 "Being a new creation means old habits and struggles disappear immediately; if they continue, the person has not really been born of God.",
 "That in union with Christ a new reality begins: God reconciles us to himself and transforms our life.",
 "God erases the past and lets us start from zero, but afterward we must build the righteousness needed to remain accepted."],
 "New creation does not mean instant perfection. It means our relationship with God and the direction of our life have been deeply renewed in Christ.",
 ["If anyone is in Christ, there is a new creation. God was reconciling the world to himself in Christ and gave us the message of reconciliation."]],
["Galatians · Gospel","Why did Paul oppose adding human requirements to the Gospel?",[
 "Faith justifies us at the beginning, but afterward religious rules are needed to keep the justification Christ began.",
 "Christ did almost everything needed to save us; our obedience to the right rules completes the part his work did not cover.",
 "Because our acceptance before God rests on Christ and cannot become a prize earned through religious requirements."],
 "Paul protects the heart of the Gospel: Christ is sufficient for our justification. Christian obedience flows from faith and the Spirit, not from trying to buy God's acceptance.",
 ["Paul teaches that a person is not justified by the works of the law, but by faith in Jesus Christ.","Christ has set us free; Paul calls us to stand firm and not return to a yoke of slavery."]],
["Galatians · Fruit","What is the fruit of the Spirit?",[
 "The character the Spirit produces in those who belong to Christ and learn to walk under his guidance.",
 "The fruit of the Spirit is the conduct we must produce to convince God that we deserve to keep his Spirit.",
 "The Spirit starts the change, but afterward the fruit depends solely on our discipline; if we fail, he stops dwelling in us."],
 "Paul speaks of fruit, not of a performance to earn God's favor. The Spirit progressively transforms the character of those who belong to Christ.",
 ["The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. Those who belong to Christ have crucified the flesh and are called to walk by the Spirit."]],
["Ephesians · Salvation","Are we saved by doing good works?",[
 "Grace covers only the part of righteousness our works fall short of; together they complete what is needed to save us.",
 "No. We are saved by grace through faith; good works are the fruit of that new life, not its price.",
 "We are saved by grace at the beginning, but afterward good works keep that salvation active before God."],
 "Ephesians holds two truths together: works do not buy salvation, but salvation produces a life prepared to do good.",
 ["You have been saved by grace through faith. This is not from yourselves: it is the gift of God, not the result of works. At the same time, we were created in Christ Jesus to do good works."]],
["Ephesians · Church","What does it mean that believers form one body in Christ?",[
 "Being one body means all true believers must hold exactly the same opinions; any disagreement shows there is no unity.",
 "The church can only be one body if all believers belong to a single human organization under one earthly authority.",
 "That Christ reconciles to God and gathers into one people those who were once separated."],
 "Christian unity does not erase all differences. Its foundation is Christ: different people are reconciled to God and called to live as one people.",
 ["Christ tore down the dividing wall of hostility and created one people, reconciling both to God through the cross.","Paul calls us to keep the unity of the Spirit: one body, one Spirit, one Lord, one faith, and one God and Father."]],
["Ephesians · Maturity","What are ministries and teaching in the church for?",[
 "To equip believers, build up the church, and help it grow toward maturity in Christ.",
 "Ministries exist so that a few leaders do the spiritual work while the other believers simply receive it.",
 "Leaders receive authority to control members' spiritual lives, because obeying the leader is necessary to keep salvation."],
 "Biblical leadership does not exist to produce spectators. Its task includes equipping God's people to serve and to grow together toward maturity.",
 ["Christ gave different servants to equip the saints for the work of ministry and to build up the body until it reaches maturity, growing in truth and love."]],
["Philippians · Humility","How does Paul present the humility of Christ?",[
 "Humility means considering ourselves worthless and thinking that any other person is necessarily better than us.",
 "Christ's humility requires always letting others decide for us; defending a boundary or a personal need is selfishness.",
 "As giving up selfishness and using our position to serve, following Christ's example."],
 "Biblical humility does not consist in despising ourselves. Christ shows an active humility: not clinging to privilege, but giving himself to serve.",
 ["Paul calls us not to act out of selfishness and to consider others. He presents Christ, who humbled himself, took the form of a servant, and was obedient to death on a cross."]],
["Philippians · Contentment","What did Paul mean when he said he could face every situation through Christ who strengthened him?",[
 "Christ promises to give us the ability to achieve any good goal we desire, as long as we have enough faith.",
 "That Christ strengthened Paul to remain faithful both in plenty and in need.",
 "Philippians 4:13 promises that a faithful believer will end up overcoming all material want and reaching prosperity."],
 "The context is about contentment. Paul does not promise unlimited success; he says Christ gives him strength to go through very different circumstances without abandoning his trust.",
 ["Paul explained that he had learned to be content both in plenty and in need. He could face all those circumstances through Christ who gave him strength."]],
["Colossians · Christ","What place does Christ hold in the Christian life?",[
 "He is the center: we receive Christ as Lord and keep growing rooted in him.",
 "Christ only opens the door of salvation; afterward we must move beyond him through higher spiritual knowledge and practices.",
 "Christ is our model, and salvation ultimately depends on how exactly we manage to imitate his life."],
 "The New Testament does not present Christ as an introduction we later leave behind. He remains at the center of our faith, growth, reconciliation and hope.",
 ["Christ is presented as supreme over creation and head of the church. Through him God reconciles all things to himself.","Just as you received Christ Jesus as Lord, continue to live in him, rooted and built up in him."]],
["1 Thessalonians · Hope","How should the hope of Christ's return affect our present life?",[
 "Preparing for Christ's return means correctly identifying the signs and calculating when it will happen so we are not caught by surprise.",
 "When he returns, Christ will receive those who have reached clean enough conduct to deserve to enter with him.",
 "It should produce hope, watchfulness, faithfulness and mutual encouragement."],
 "Future hope does not invite obsessive speculation about dates. Paul connects it with sobriety, faithfulness, comfort and care among believers.",
 ["Paul teaches that believers do not face death without hope, because Jesus died and rose again, and God will bring with him those who belong to him.","In light of the day of the Lord, Paul calls us to stay awake, live with faith, love and hope, and encourage one another."]],
["Pastoral letters · Scripture","What is Scripture useful for?",[
 "Scripture contains the rules we must keep to produce our salvation and make ourselves worthy of Christ.",
 "To lead us to the wisdom of salvation in Christ, teach us, correct us, and prepare us to live and serve.",
 "Scripture automatically transforms whoever reads it, even if that person rejects Christ and does not believe its message."],
 "Scripture was not given as religious decoration or as a storehouse of isolated phrases. God uses it to lead us to Christ, teach us, and shape our life.",
 ["The Holy Scriptures are able to make one wise for salvation through faith in Christ. All Scripture is God-breathed and useful for teaching, correcting and training in righteousness, equipping God's servant for every good work."]],
["Pastoral letters · Money","What does the New Testament teach about the love of money?",[
 "That the love of money can enslave the heart; we are called to contentment and to put our hope in God.",
 "Financial prosperity is a normal sign of God's approval; a faithful believer should expect to become materially prosperous.",
 "Money itself is sinful; that is why owning more than what is strictly necessary is incompatible with a godly life."],
 "Paul does not say money itself is the root of all evil. He warns about the love of money and the desire that ends up ruling a person.",
 ["Godliness with contentment is great gain. The desire to get rich can become a trap, and the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil."]],
["Hebrews · Christ's priesthood","Why can we approach God with confidence?",[
 "Jesus opened access to God, but we can keep approaching him only as long as our conduct stays clean enough.",
 "Because Jesus is our great high priest, and through his work we have access to the throne of grace.",
 "Jesus' sacrifice and our obedience each provide part of the righteousness needed to come before God."],
 "Our confidence does not come from believing we are flawless. Precisely because we need mercy, we draw near trusting in Jesus and in what he has done.",
 ["We have a great high priest, Jesus, who can sympathize with our weaknesses. That is why we are invited to approach the throne of grace to receive mercy and help.","Through Jesus' work we have confidence to enter and draw near to God with a sincere heart and full assurance of faith."]],
["Hebrews · Faith","What is faith according to the example of Hebrews 11?",[
 "Faith is convincing ourselves intensely that what we want will happen; a strong enough conviction makes it happen.",
 "God's promises are received when our obedience reaches the necessary level; if they are not fulfilled it is because our faith was still insufficient.",
 "Trusting God and his promises in such a way that this trust directs our steps."],
 "Biblical faith has an object: God and his word. It is not mental power to make our wishes come true; it is trust in God expressed in obedience.",
 ["Faith trusts in what God has promised even though it is not yet seen. Whoever comes to God believes that he exists and that he rewards those who seek him.","Abraham obeyed God's call without knowing the whole way in advance, looking forward to what God had promised."]],
["Hebrews · Perseverance","How should we run the race of faith?",[
 "Looking to Jesus, throwing off what hinders us, and persevering.",
 "Persevering means keeping enough obedience to prevent God from withdrawing the salvation he first granted us.",
 "Perseverance ultimately depends on our discipline; Jesus can help us, but we are the ones who guarantee reaching the end."],
 "Christian perseverance is not fueled by competing with others. Hebrews directs our eyes to Jesus and calls us to remove whatever keeps us from moving forward.",
 ["We are called to throw off the weight and the sin that hinder us, run with perseverance, and keep our eyes on Jesus, who endured the cross."]],
["James · Faith and works","What does James mean when he says that faith without works is dead?",[
 "James teaches that faith provides part of our justification and works provide the rest.",
 "Works turn a faith that does not yet save into saving faith; God justifies us when both together reach what is needed.",
 "That true faith does not remain only in words, but produces visible fruit in life."],
 "James does not present works as the price of salvation. He confronts an empty profession of faith: when faith is real, it begins to show in the way we live.",
 ["James asks what good it is to claim to have faith if that faith never leads to action. He uses the example of seeing a brother in need and offering only words.","As the body without the spirit is dead, so faith without works is dead."]],
["James · Trials","How can a believer face trials?",[
 "Every trial comes directly from God and we must stay in it; trying to get out of a harmful situation means resisting his will.",
 "By seeking wisdom from God and letting the trial produce perseverance.",
 "If a trial continues after praying, it usually means we still do not have enough faith for God to remove it."],
 "The Bible does not require us to pretend that pain is pleasant. James teaches that God can use trials to build perseverance, and invites us to ask for wisdom.",
 ["James teaches that trials can produce perseverance. If anyone lacks wisdom, they should ask God, who gives generously.","Blessed is the one who perseveres under trial, because having stood the test, that person will receive the crown of life promised to those who love God."]],
["1 Peter · New birth","What hope does the new birth produce?",[
 "A living hope based on Jesus' resurrection and on the inheritance God keeps for his people.",
 "The new birth only gives us the possibility of receiving an inheritance; keeping it ultimately depends on building up enough faithfulness.",
 "A living hope means God will necessarily improve our present circumstances as evidence that we are truly his children."],
 "Peter writes to believers facing hardship. Their hope did not depend on a comfortable life, but on Christ's resurrection and on what God had prepared.",
 ["God, in his great mercy, has given us new birth into a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ, and into an inheritance that can never perish."]],
["1 Peter · Holiness","What does it mean to live in holiness?",[
 "Being holy means avoiding contact with non-Christians as much as possible, because relating to them makes us impure before God.",
 "Holiness consists in keeping outward rules that set us apart; as long as we keep those rules, the state of the heart is secondary.",
 "Belonging to God and letting that new identity transform the way we live."],
 "Holiness is not religious superiority. God calls those who belong to him to reflect his character in their conduct.",
 ["Peter calls us to set our hope fully on grace and not to live according to our former desires. Since God is holy, his people are also called to be holy in all they do."]],
["1 Peter · Suffering","What should a Christian do when suffering for doing what is right?",[
 "Following Christ's example means never seeking protection or justice when we suffer abuse; defending ourselves would be a lack of faith.",
 "Keep doing good, trust in God's justice, and respond with conduct that honors Christ.",
 "All of a believer's suffering is sent directly by God, so trying to change an unjust situation means resisting his purpose."],
 "Peter does not say that all suffering is just or that we should seek it. He teaches how to respond when we suffer precisely for doing good: without abandoning faithfulness or becoming like what hurts us.",
 ["Peter points to Christ, who suffered unjustly without responding with threats, but entrusting himself to him who judges justly.","Believers must be ready to explain their hope with gentleness and respect, keeping a clear conscience."]],
["2 Peter · Growth","How does Peter describe Christian growth?",[
 "As growth in which God's grace produces an increasingly mature and fruitful life.",
 "Christian growth consists in gaining enough knowledge and discipline to reach a point where we no longer struggle with sin.",
 "Christian virtues complete what faith lacks to save us; without reaching enough virtues God will finally reject us."],
 "Christian knowledge must reach our life. Peter connects faith with character, self-control, perseverance and love.",
 ["God has given us everything we need for a life that pleases him. Peter calls us to add to faith goodness, knowledge, self-control, perseverance, godliness, brotherly affection and love."]],
["Gospels · Grace","What did Jesus mean to teach with the parable of the workers who received the same pay?",[
 "That God rewards exactly according to the number of years a person has served him.",
 "That God's generosity does not work like wages we can demand for our merits.",
 "That everyone will receive exactly the same responsibilities and rewards no matter how they have lived."],
 "The parable confronts the idea of turning grace into wages. God is generous, and no one can come before him as if his favor were a debt he owed.",
 ["Jesus told of a landowner who hired workers at different hours of the day and at the end gave all of them what he had decided to give. Some complained because they expected to receive more than the others."]],
["Gospels · Faith","What do we learn from the father who said, “I believe; help my unbelief”?",[
 "That true faith can never coexist with questions or weakness.",
 "That we can come to Jesus with real faith even while we still struggle with doubts and need his help.",
 "That doubt strengthens faith by itself, so we do not need to look for answers."],
 "Faith does not need to fake a certainty it does not have. This man brought even his weakness to Jesus. The answer was not to abandon faith, but to ask for help in the middle of it.",
 ["The father asked Jesus to help his son. When Jesus spoke about believing, the man answered honestly: he believed, but recognized that his faith needed help."]],
["Gospels · Tradition","What problem did Jesus point out when a religious tradition takes the place of God's Word?",[
 "That every religious tradition is necessarily against God.",
 "That a tradition can be useful, but must never have authority to correct, replace or nullify what God has said.",
 "That ancient traditions have authority as long as they have been practiced for long enough."],
 "Jesus did not condemn something simply for being traditional. The problem arises when a human custom is given an authority that belongs to God's Word.",
 ["Jesus rebuked those who honored God with their lips while elevating human traditions in such a way that they ended up nullifying God's command."]],
["Gospels · Heart","According to Jesus, where does human moral defilement really come from?",[
 "Mainly from the environment and the bad influences surrounding the person.",
 "From the human heart; that is why we need something deeper than outward changes.",
 "From not knowing enough rules to correctly tell good from evil."],
 "Jesus takes the problem all the way to the heart. Surroundings have influence and teaching matters, but our need is deeper than fixing outward circumstances.",
 ["Jesus taught that from within, out of the human heart, come evil thoughts, immorality, deceit, pride and other evils."]],
["Gospels · Mercy","What did Jesus mean by “I desire mercy, not sacrifice”?",[
 "That religious practices have no value and can be abandoned.",
 "That performing religious acts while despising those in need can reveal that we have not understood God's heart.",
 "That showing mercy to others makes up before God for the sins we have committed."],
 "Jesus confronts an outward religion that can carry out ceremonies while losing sight of mercy. Mercy does not buy forgiveness, but it reveals an understanding of God's character.",
 ["When Jesus was criticized for eating with sinners, he reminded them that the sick need a doctor and quoted: I desire mercy, not sacrifice. He said he came to call sinners."]],
["Gospels · Fruit","What did Jesus mean when he taught that a tree is known by its fruit?",[
 "That a person can know for certain another person's eternal destiny by observing a few mistakes.",
 "That over time, a life and a teaching reveal what a person is really producing.",
 "That a true believer will always show perfect conduct without contradictions."],
 "Jesus does not invite us to judge hastily over a single failure. He speaks of fruit: what a life and a teaching recognizably produce.",
 ["Jesus warned about false prophets and said they would be recognized by their fruit, in the same way that a tree is recognized by what it produces."]],
["Gospels · Treasure","What does it mean to store up treasures in heaven?",[
 "Giving up all material possessions, because having goods on earth prevents us from serving God.",
 "Living in a way that values what belongs to God's kingdom instead of making temporary things our security and main purpose.",
 "Doing enough good works to build up merits that God will use to decide our entry into heaven."],
 "Jesus points to the heart and to what we consider truly valuable. He does not teach us to buy heaven with works, but to live with priorities that do not end with what is temporary.",
 ["Jesus contrasted treasures on earth, which can be lost, with treasures in heaven. He added that where our treasure is, there our heart will be also."]],
["Gospels · Anxiety","When Jesus says “do not worry”, is he teaching that we should never plan?",[
 "Yes. Planning for the future shows that we do not yet fully trust God.",
 "No. Jesus confronts the anxiety that controls us; we can act responsibly while depending on the Father's care.",
 "We should only plan spiritual matters, because material needs must be left entirely in God's hands."],
 "Jesus does not condemn responsibility. He teaches us not to live ruled by a worry that tries to carry today all of tomorrow's problems.",
 ["Jesus spoke about worry over food, drink and clothing. He reminded them of the Father's care and called them to seek first the kingdom of God, facing each day without carrying the next one ahead of time."]],
["Gospels · Judging","What does “do not judge” mean in Matthew 7?",[
 "That we can never say a behavior or teaching is wrong.",
 "That we must avoid hypocritical judgment and examine ourselves first, without abandoning the discernment we need.",
 "That only those who have no sin at all can correct another person."],
 "The same sermon that says ‘do not judge’ also commands discernment. Jesus condemns hypocrisy and a proud measuring stick, not all moral or doctrinal evaluation.",
 ["Jesus warned against hypocritical judging: seeing a small fault in another while ignoring a much bigger one in ourselves. First we must examine ourselves, and then we can help our brother.","Shortly afterward Jesus commands us to discern false prophets by their fruit."]],
["Gospels · Commandments","What does it mean to love God with all our heart and our neighbor as ourselves?",[
 "That if we feel sincere love, the other commandments no longer matter.",
 "That obedience to God finds its right direction in love for God and in seeking our neighbor's good.",
 "That loving our neighbor is enough even if our relationship with God does not matter."],
 "Jesus does not set love against obedience. He shows the heart that God's will aims at: true love for God and for our neighbor.",
 ["Jesus said the greatest commandment is to love God with all our being, and the second is to love our neighbor as ourselves. All the Law and the Prophets hang on these."]],
["Gospels · Lord's Supper","What does the Lord's Supper proclaim about Jesus?",[
 "Mainly the unity and friendship among those who share a Christian meal.",
 "Jesus' self-giving for us and the new covenant established through his death.",
 "That taking the bread and the cup automatically produces forgiveness, even without faith."],
 "The Supper turns our eyes to Christ given for us. It is not only fellowship, nor an automatic act separated from faith.",
 ["Jesus took bread and wine and connected them with his body given for us and with the new covenant established through his blood."]],
["Gospels · Spirit","What does the Holy Spirit do according to Jesus' words in John?",[
 "He directs attention mainly to the spiritual experiences of the person who receives him.",
 "He works to show the truth about sin and about Christ, guiding the disciples and glorifying Jesus.",
 "He brings new teachings that can replace Jesus' words when the church needs to adapt."],
 "In John, the Spirit's work is deeply centered on Christ. He does not compete with Jesus or displace his word: he glorifies Christ and leads into the truth.",
 ["Jesus said the Spirit would convict the world and guide his disciples into the truth, glorifying Christ and making known what he receives from him."]],
["Gospels · Remaining","What does it mean to remain in Christ, according to John 15?",[
 "Keeping a constant spiritual discipline to preserve the life we first received from Christ.",
 "Living in continual dependence on Christ, because the fruit of the Christian life comes from our union with him.",
 "Keeping a strong emotion toward Jesus to avoid periods of spiritual dryness."],
 "Remaining describes dependence, fellowship and continuity in Christ. Fruit does not come from religious independence, but from a life that receives from him.",
 ["Jesus compared himself to the vine and his disciples to the branches. A branch cannot bear fruit apart from the vine; apart from him they cannot bear the fruit God is looking for."]],
["Gospels · Service","What does Jesus teach by washing his disciples' feet?",[
 "That foot washing is the main work by which we show humility before God.",
 "That no position among God's people places us above humble service to others.",
 "That a Christian leader must never exercise authority or make hard decisions."],
 "Jesus does not abolish leadership, but he destroys the idea that leadership means personal superiority. The Lord himself took the servant's place.",
 ["After washing their feet, Jesus explained that, being Teacher and Lord, he had served them this way and left them an example so they would also serve one another."]],
["Gospels · Eternal life","What is eternal life according to Jesus' prayer in John 17?",[
 "Living forever, regardless of our relationship with God.",
 "Knowing the Father through Jesus Christ; it is a life in relationship with God that begins now and continues forever.",
 "Gaining enough knowledge about God to be ready for the life to come."],
 "Eternal life includes duration, but Jesus describes it relationally: knowing the Father and the Son. It is not simply endless existence.",
 ["Jesus spoke of eternal life as knowing the only true God and Jesus Christ, whom he sent."]],
["Acts · Berea","Why were the believers in Berea commended?",[
 "Because they immediately accepted the apostles' teaching without questioning it.",
 "Because they listened eagerly and at the same time checked the teaching in light of the Scriptures.",
 "Because they distrusted every teacher and only accepted what they could discover without help."],
 "A teachable attitude does not require gullibility. The Bereans listened seriously and checked. It is a good model of humility and discernment.",
 ["They received the message with eagerness and examined the Scriptures every day to see whether what they heard was true."]],
["Acts · Simon","What mistake did Simon make when he wanted to obtain God's power with money?",[
 "He thought God's gifts could become something to acquire and control for personal gain.",
 "He thought he had to support the ministry financially before he could receive a spiritual responsibility.",
 "He wanted to receive a gift before proving for long enough that he was ready to use it."],
 "The problem was not simply money. Simon treated God's gift as power to be purchased and used. Grace and the Spirit are not merchandise.",
 ["Simon offered the apostles money to receive spiritual authority. Peter rebuked him because he thought God's gift could be bought."]],
["Acts · Cornelius","What did Peter learn in Cornelius' house?",[
 "That a sincere person can be saved without Christ if they do enough good works.",
 "That God does not limit the Gospel to one ethnic group; people from all nations are called to receive Christ.",
 "That cultural differences cease to exist when someone becomes a Christian."],
 "The episode breaks down an ethnic barrier; it does not remove Christ. Peter precisely proclaims Jesus and forgiveness through his name.",
 ["Peter recognized that God had shown him he should not call any person unclean and that God does not show favoritism among peoples.","Peter proclaimed forgiveness through Jesus, and the Holy Spirit came on those who were listening."]],
["Acts · Discernment","What does the Jerusalem council show about resolving a doctrinal controversy?",[
 "That every controversy should be settled simply by majority vote.",
 "That the church must listen, consider God's work, and submit its conclusions to the testimony of Scripture.",
 "That the leaders with the most authority can decide doctrine without explaining their reasons from the Bible."],
 "Acts shows community deliberation and attention to evidence, but the decision does not rest simply on preferences. Scripture and God's work are central.",
 ["The apostles and elders met to consider the dispute over requiring circumcision of the Gentiles. They heard testimonies, remembered God's work and examined the Scriptures before reaching a conclusion."]],
["Romans · Obedience","If we are not justified by works, why does obeying God matter?",[
 "Because obedience preserves the justification we first received by faith.",
 "Because obedience is the response to and fruit of the mercy we have received, not the price with which we buy our acceptance.",
 "Because works are ultimately added to faith to complete justification."],
 "Romans does not set grace against obedience. Grace changes the root of obedience: we no longer try to buy acceptance, but respond to God's mercy.",
 ["Paul rejects the idea of sinning because we are under grace. Those who were set free from sin have come to a new obedience from the heart.","In view of God's mercies, Paul calls us to offer our lives to God and be transformed by the renewing of the mind."]],
["Romans · Conscience","What does Romans 14 teach when sincere believers reach different conclusions on disputable matters?",[
 "That every doctrinal difference should be treated as unimportant.",
 "That in matters that do not contradict the Gospel we must avoid looking down on one another and act with conscience, love and mutual consideration.",
 "That the one with the freer conscience must always give in, because their position is less spiritual."],
 "Paul does not declare all doctrine secondary. He deals with specific matters in which believers could honor the Lord in different ways and calls for living together in love.",
 ["Paul speaks of believers who differed about food and days. He commands them to accept one another without quarreling to condemn, since each seeks to honor the Lord.","Paul calls us not to put a stumbling block in a brother's way and to pursue what leads to peace and mutual building up."]],
["Romans · Transformation","What does it mean to be transformed by the renewing of our mind?",[
 "Learning enough biblical teaching until we no longer need to struggle with old patterns.",
 "Letting God's truth change the way we think and, with it, the way we live and discern.",
 "Rejecting every thought or custom that comes from people who are not Christians."],
 "Renewing the mind is not piling up information or automatically rejecting everything cultural. It is a transformation of the way we think under God's truth.",
 ["Paul calls us not to conform to this world, but to be transformed by the renewing of the mind so that we can discern God's will."]],
["1 Corinthians · Freedom","How should a Christian use their freedom when a decision could harm another spiritually?",[
 "If something is permitted, we should do it to teach others to have a stronger conscience.",
 "Christian freedom is governed by love; sometimes we may willingly give up a right for the good of another.",
 "We must always submit to the strictest conscience of any person to avoid all disagreement."],
 "Freedom is not selfishness with Christian vocabulary. But neither does it mean being permanently ruled by the strictest rule. Paul asks for love and building one another up.",
 ["Paul warns that one person's freedom must not become a stumbling block for the weak. He is willing to give up something permitted rather than wound a brother's conscience.","Not everything permitted is beneficial or constructive; no one should seek only their own good, but also the good of others."]],
["1 Corinthians · Discipline","What is discipline within the church for?",[
 "To protect the holiness of the community and seek restoration, not to enjoy punishment or display superiority.",
 "To show publicly which people have lost their salvation and separate them permanently.",
 "To maintain the church's reputation by keeping internal problems from becoming known."],
 "Biblical discipline takes sin seriously, but its spirit is not revenge. Correction and restoration must go together.",
 ["Paul confronts a serious sin the community was tolerating and calls them to act instead of being proud of their tolerance.","In another case of discipline, Paul asks that, after sufficient correction, they forgive, comfort and reaffirm their love."]],
["2 Corinthians · Weakness","What did Paul learn when he asked for his “thorn” to be taken away?",[
 "That if we pray enough times, God will finally remove whatever makes us suffer.",
 "That Christ's grace can sustain us even when God does not remove a weakness or difficulty we have asked him to take away.",
 "That we should stop asking God to change hard circumstances, because doing so shows little faith."],
 "Paul did ask for the difficulty to be removed. What he learned was that an answer different from what he expected does not mean an absence of grace.",
 ["Paul asked three times for it to be taken away. The Lord's answer was that his grace was sufficient and that his power is made perfect in weakness."]],
["2 Corinthians · Generosity","How does Paul describe the Christian way of giving?",[
 "Giving a large enough amount to prove our trust and receive a proportional blessing.",
 "Giving willingly and generously, not under manipulation or pressure, trusting God.",
 "Always giving the same percentage, because any smaller amount turns the offering into disobedience."],
 "Paul speaks of generosity, but also of a decision of the heart and the absence of coercion. Giving is not buying a return from God.",
 ["Paul calls us to give as we have decided in our heart, not reluctantly or under compulsion, because God loves a cheerful giver."]],
["Galatians · Freedom","What does Christian freedom mean in Galatians?",[
 "That there are no longer moral commands for those who live under grace.",
 "That we have been set free from slavery to live by the Spirit and serve in love, not to live ruled by our desires.",
 "That we are free as long as we keep, through our obedience, the right to remain under grace."],
 "The freedom of Galatians is not autonomy to do anything. It is freedom from slavery and from justification by law, for a life led by the Spirit.",
 ["Paul says they were called to freedom, but not to use it as an opportunity for the flesh; through love they should serve one another."]],
["Galatians · Restoration","How should a believer caught in a sin be treated?",[
 "They should be corrected with public firmness so that others understand the seriousness of sin.",
 "They should be restored gently, without ignoring the sin and without forgetting our own vulnerability.",
 "It is better not to step in, because any correction would be judging another person's life."],
 "Paul avoids two extremes: indifference and superiority. The goal is restoration, and the one who helps must remember that they too can be tempted.",
 ["Those who are spiritual should restore the one who has fallen with a spirit of gentleness, watching themselves too, and carrying each other's burdens."]],
["Ephesians · Forgiveness","What is the basis for forgiving one another?",[
 "We forgive so that God sees our mercy and then decides to forgive us.",
 "The forgiveness we have received in Christ becomes the basis and model for forgiving others.",
 "Forgiving means immediately restoring the same trust that existed before the offense."],
 "Christian forgiveness flows from the forgiveness we have received. That does not mean damaged trust is always rebuilt instantly or that all consequences disappear.",
 ["Paul calls us to get rid of bitterness, anger and malice, and to be kind and compassionate, forgiving one another as God forgave us in Christ."]],
["Ephesians · Spiritual warfare","What does it mean to put on the armor of God?",[
 "Learning special techniques to identify which spirit is behind each everyday problem.",
 "Standing firm in truth, righteousness, the Gospel, faith, salvation, the Word and prayer in the face of evil.",
 "Keeping a sinless life to prevent any spiritual force from coming near us."],
 "The image of the armor does not point to secret practices, but to central realities of the Christian life: truth, the Gospel, faith, the Word and prayer.",
 ["Paul describes truth, righteousness, the gospel of peace, faith, salvation, the word of God and prayer as the armor with which believers stand firm."]],
["Philippians · Anxiety","What does Paul propose in the face of anxiety in Philippians 4?",[
 "Ignoring worrying thoughts and repeating that everything will be fine until we feel peace.",
 "Bringing our worries to God in prayer and learning to direct the mind toward what is true and good.",
 "Considering anxiety as evidence that our faith is not genuine."],
 "Paul does not shame the believer for experiencing worry. He directs them toward God, prayer, gratitude, and a mind shaped by the truth.",
 ["Paul calls us to present our requests to God with prayer and thanksgiving. He also encourages us to set our minds on what is true, right, pure and worthy."]],
["Colossians · Rules","Why does Paul warn against religious rules that look very spiritual but do not transform the heart?",[
 "Because any practical rule within the Christian life is legalism.",
 "Because outward discipline can have an appearance of holiness without solving, by itself, the inner problem of sin.",
 "Because once we believe, controlling our desires is no longer important."],
 "Paul does not condemn all discipline. He warns against turning human rules and outward severity into the power that supposedly transforms the heart.",
 ["Paul mentions rules such as ‘do not handle, do not taste, do not touch’. He says they have an appearance of wisdom and severity, but no real power against the desires of the flesh."]],
["1 Thessalonians · Discernment","What does “test everything; hold on to what is good” mean?",[
 "First accepting every spiritual experience and questioning it only if it causes some visible harm.",
 "Not automatically rejecting what is spiritual, but not accepting it without discernment either; we must test it and hold on to what is good.",
 "Distrusting every spiritual manifestation, because the only safe way to avoid error is to reject it."],
 "Paul joins openness and discernment. Neither gullibility nor automatic rejection: whatever presents itself as spiritual must be tested.",
 ["Paul says not to quench the Spirit or despise prophecies, but he also commands them to test everything, hold on to what is good, and reject every kind of evil."]],
["2 Thessalonians · Work","What problem is Paul correcting when he says that whoever is unwilling to work should not eat?",[
 "He teaches that every unemployed person should be left without help until they find work.",
 "He corrects those who deliberately refuse to work when they are able to; he does not condemn those who cannot work or need help.",
 "He teaches that receiving help from other believers always creates dependence and should be avoided."],
 "The text says ‘unwilling’, not ‘unable’. It must not be used to condemn the sick, the vulnerable, or those going through real need.",
 ["Paul refers to people who, though able, were unwilling to work and lived in idleness, meddling in others' affairs. He commands them to work quietly to earn their own food."]],
["1 Timothy · Leadership","What kind of character does the New Testament look for in a church leader?",[
 "Mainly the ability to speak, attract people, and make the congregation grow quickly.",
 "Proven character, ability to teach, and a way of leading that sets an example without lording it over the flock.",
 "A strong authority that makes members obey even when they do not understand the decisions."],
 "The New Testament devotes a surprising amount of space to character. Talent does not replace integrity, service and example.",
 ["Paul highlights sobriety, self-control, hospitality, ability to teach, managing one's family well, not being violent, and a good reputation.","Peter asks leaders to shepherd the flock willingly, not out of greed and without lording it over those in their care, but being examples."]],
["2 Timothy · Teaching","How should a servant of the Lord correct those who oppose him?",[
 "He should respond more harshly to keep the error from seeming acceptable.",
 "He should teach clearly and gently, remembering that a change of heart depends on God's work.",
 "He should avoid correcting false doctrines because every discussion produces division."],
 "Gentleness does not require doctrinal weakness. Paul combines real correction with patience and dependence on God to bring about repentance.",
 ["Paul says the Lord's servant must not be quarrelsome, but kind, able to teach, patient, and ready to correct gently, hoping that God will grant repentance."]],
["Titus · Grace","According to Titus 2, what does grace do besides offering us salvation?",[
 "It forgives us when we fail, but changed behavior depends on our effort to keep that forgiveness.",
 "It also trains us: the grace that saves shapes a new life while we wait for Christ.",
 "It makes any moral teaching unnecessary, because living under grace means acting only according to conscience."],
 "Grace is not only an eraser for the past. Paul says it also teaches us. Salvation and transformation are not enemies.",
 ["The grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation and teaching us to renounce ungodliness and to live sensible, upright and godly lives while we wait for Christ."]],
["Philemon · Brotherhood","What does the Gospel change in the relationship between Philemon and Onesimus?",[
 "The Gospel only changes the spiritual relationship; human relationships need not be affected.",
 "The new identity in Christ transforms the way we see and receive another person, even within deeply unequal social relationships.",
 "Conversion automatically abolishes all existing social structures at that moment."],
 "Paul brings the identity of brother straight into a concrete relationship. The Gospel is not locked inside; it changes how we see and treat people.",
 ["Paul asks that Onesimus be received no longer simply as a slave, but as something much greater: a dear brother. He asks Philemon to welcome him as he would welcome Paul himself."]],
["Hebrews · New covenant","What does it mean that Jesus is the mediator of a new covenant?",[
 "That Jesus added new teachings to the former covenant so we could keep it better.",
 "That through Christ God establishes the promised relationship, brings forgiveness, and works inwardly in his people.",
 "That the new covenant means God no longer expects any obedience from his people."],
 "The new covenant is not simply a better list of rules. It includes forgiveness, relationship with God, and an inner work that transforms his people.",
 ["Hebrews presents Jesus as mediator of a better covenant and recalls God's promise to write his law on the heart, to be his people's God, and to forgive their sins.","Christ is the mediator of a new covenant so that those who are called may receive the promised inheritance."]],
["Hebrews · Meeting together","Why does Hebrews encourage believers not to stop meeting together?",[
 "Because missing a meeting automatically puts our salvation in danger.",
 "Because we need to encourage one another, spur one another on to love, and persevere together in hope.",
 "Because attending meetings is the main evidence that a person is a Christian."],
 "The text does not present attendance as a token to earn salvation. Community matters because we need one another's encouragement, love and perseverance.",
 ["We are called to hold firmly to our hope, consider how to spur one another on toward love and good deeds, not give up meeting together, and encourage one another."]],
["James · The Word","What does it mean to be a doer of the Word and not merely a hearer?",[
 "That listening to biblical teaching has little value and only our actions matter.",
 "That truly receiving the Word means letting what we hear reach the way we live.",
 "That putting the Word into practice is what makes God's message true for us."],
 "James does not set hearing against doing. He confronts hearing without responding. The Word must be received in a way that transforms practice.",
 ["James compares someone who hears but does not practice to a person who looks at their face in a mirror and then forgets what they look like. Whoever perseveres in the word and practices it is called blessed."]],
["James · The tongue","Why does James give so much importance to our words?",[
 "Because words are more serious sins than physical actions.",
 "Because our words have great power to steer, build up or destroy, and they reveal deep contradictions of the heart.",
 "Because a mature believer should never express disagreement directly."],
 "James does not ask for permanent silence or avoid disagreement. He makes us take seriously the moral power of the way we speak.",
 ["James compares the tongue to a small rudder and to a fire: something small can steer or damage a great deal. With the same mouth we can praise God and hurt people made in his likeness."]],
["1 Peter · Defending the faith","How should we explain our hope to those who ask us?",[
 "With overwhelming arguments that leave our opponent with no reply.",
 "With preparation and clarity, but also with gentleness, respect and consistent conduct.",
 "By avoiding difficult questions so as not to get into discussions that might weaken our faith."],
 "Defending the faith is not a contest to humiliate the other person. Peter joins content, attitude and conduct.",
 ["Peter calls us to be ready to give an answer for our hope, but to do it with gentleness and respect, keeping a clear conscience."]],
["2 Peter · Scripture","What does Peter warn about interpreting the Scriptures?",[
 "That any sincere interpretation is valid as long as it helps the person spiritually.",
 "That some passages are hard and can be twisted; we need humility, stability and care in interpreting them.",
 "That only specialists can understand the Bible and other believers should not interpret it."],
 "Peter acknowledges real difficulty without declaring Scripture inaccessible. The answer to hard passages is care and growth, not inventing meanings or giving up reading.",
 ["Peter acknowledges that Paul's letters contain some things hard to understand, which ignorant and unstable people distort, as they do the other Scriptures, to their own destruction."]],
["1 John · Sin","How can John say the believer must not live in sin and at the same time teach that they can still sin?",[
 "John teaches that true believers stop sinning completely; 1 John 1 refers only to unconverted people.",
 "John distinguishes between acknowledging that we still sin and living with sin as our practice and identity without repentance.",
 "As long as we confess our sins, it doesn't matter if we decide to keep practicing them."],
 "John rejects both perfectionism and a faith indifferent to sin. The believer still needs forgiveness, but the life born of God changes their relationship with sin.",
 ["John says that claiming to be without sin is deceiving ourselves; if we sin, we have an advocate with the Father: Jesus Christ.","John also teaches that whoever remains in Christ cannot make sin their normal practice, because the life born of God produces a different direction."]],
["1 John · Truth and love","Can Christian love be separated from the truth?",[
 "Yes. Loving means accepting another person's decisions even when we know they may harm them.",
 "No. Biblical love seeks real good and walks together with truth and obedience to God.",
 "No. That is why telling the truth is always loving, no matter the manner or intention with which it is said."],
 "Love without truth can turn into indifference, and truth without love can be used as a weapon. John keeps the two together.",
 ["John calls us not to love only with words, but with actions and in truth.","John joins love and obedience: loving means walking according to God's commands."]],
["3 John · Hospitality","What does 3 John teach about supporting those who serve the Gospel?",[
 "That every Christian preacher must receive financial support without any need to examine their teaching.",
 "That faithfully helping those who serve Christ can make us fellow workers for the truth.",
 "That only those who preach publicly truly take part in the church's mission."],
 "Not everyone has the same role. John shows that faithfully welcoming and supporting those who serve is also taking part in the work.",
 ["John commends the faithfulness shown in welcoming and helping brothers who went out for the sake of Christ's name. He says that by supporting them we work together for the truth."]],
["Jude · Grace","What error does Jude denounce in those who turn grace into a license to sin?",[
 "Thinking that grace is enough to save without adding our works as a guarantee.",
 "Using grace as an excuse to live deliberately against the Lord we claim to follow.",
 "Believing that God can forgive serious sins when there is true repentance."],
 "Jude does not correct the greatness of grace, but its distortion. Grace does not make sin good or remove Christ's lordship.",
 ["Jude warns about people who slipped in and perverted the grace of God into a license for immorality, denying with their lives our only Lord."]],
["Revelation · Churches","What does Jesus' message to the seven churches show?",[
 "That a true church always receives Jesus' full approval even if it has serious problems.",
 "That Christ truly knows his churches, can recognize their faithfulness, and can also confront them when they need to repent.",
 "That the problems of those churches belonged only to the first century and teach nothing to later churches."],
 "The letters show Christ present and evaluating his people. Christ's love does not keep him from correcting, and correction does not mean he ignores faithfulness.",
 ["Jesus knows the works, sufferings, faithfulness, sins and dangers of the churches. He praises what is good, confronts what is wrong, and calls them to repent and persevere."]],
["Revelation · Worship","Why does worship belong only to God and to the Lamb?",[
 "Because angels have less spiritual power and therefore receive a lesser form of veneration.",
 "Because worship belongs to God; even heavenly messengers refuse to take that place.",
 "Because any expression of respect toward a person or authority amounts to worship."],
 "Revelation draws a clear line: even a mighty angel refuses worship. The Lamb, by contrast, shares in heaven's worship.",
 ["All creation ascribes praise, honor, glory and power to the one seated on the throne and to the Lamb.","When John tries to fall down before the angel, the angel stops him and tells him: worship God."]]

];
